= Solution
{parent=i}

Inside the <infinite square well>, the stationary <wavefunctions> can be chosen real:
$$
\boxed{\Psi_n(x)=C_n\sin\frac{n\pi x}{a},\qquad n=1,2,3,\ldots,\quad 0<x<a.}
$$
They vanish outside the well and at its impenetrable endpoints. A sine meets the left boundary condition; the right one requires $\sin(n\pi)=0$. The putative $n=0$ function is identically zero, so it is not a <quantum state>. The constants $C_n$ are fixed by <wavefunction normalization>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

<Wavefunction normalization> requires unit total probability:
$$
1=\int_0^a|\Psi_n(x)|^2\,\mathrm dx
=|C_n|^2\int_0^a\frac{1-\cos(2n\pi x/a)}2\,\mathrm dx
=|C_n|^2\frac a2.
$$
Choosing an irrelevant overall phase to be zero gives
$$
\boxed{\Psi_n(x)=\sqrt{\frac2a}\sin\frac{n\pi x}{a}.}
$$
The amplitude has units of inverse square root length, so $|\Psi_n|^2\,\mathrm dx$ is dimensionless.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Within the well, the potential is zero and the <Hamiltonian (quantum mechanics)> is purely kinetic:
$$
\boxed{\hat H=-\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\frac{\mathrm d^2}{\mathrm dx^2}.}
$$
The infinite walls are encoded in the operator's boundary conditions $\Psi(0)=\Psi(a)=0$, rather than differentiated as part of the interior expression. Since
$$
\Psi_n''(x)=-\left(\frac{n\pi}{a}\right)^2\Psi_n(x),
$$
we obtain
$$
\hat H\Psi_n=\boxed{\frac{n^2\pi^2\hbar^2}{2ma^2}}\Psi_n,
\qquad \boxed{E_n=\frac{n^2h^2}{8ma^2}}.
$$
These positive <energy eigenvalues> grow quadratically with the <quantum number>; the <ground state> has a nonzero confinement energy.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For distinct positive integers $n,m$, use the product-to-sum identity in the <inner product>:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\langle\Psi_m|\Psi_n\rangle
&=\frac2a\int_0^a\sin\frac{m\pi x}{a}\sin\frac{n\pi x}{a}\,\mathrm dx\\
&=\frac1a\int_0^a\left[\cos\frac{(m-n)\pi x}{a}-\cos\frac{(m+n)\pi x}{a}\right]\,\mathrm dx\\
&=\left[\frac{\sin((m-n)\pi x/a)}{(m-n)\pi}-\frac{\sin((m+n)\pi x/a)}{(m+n)\pi}\right]_0^a=0.
\end{aligned}
$$
Every endpoint sine vanishes. For $m=n$, the normalization integral is one; the expression with a denominator $m-n$ should not be substituted directly. Thus **the normalized functions form an orthonormal set**, $\langle\Psi_m|\Psi_n\rangle=\delta_{mn}$.

= v
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

Project the expansion onto one normalized basis function. <Orthogonality> removes every other coefficient:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_0^a\Psi_m(x)^*f(x)\,\mathrm dx
&=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}a_n\int_0^a\Psi_m(x)^*\Psi_n(x)\,\mathrm dx\\
&=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}a_n\delta_{mn}=a_m.
\end{aligned}
$$
Consequently,
$$
\boxed{a_n=\langle\Psi_n|f\rangle
=\sqrt{\frac2a}\int_0^a f(x)\sin\frac{n\pi x}{a}\,\mathrm dx.}
$$
This is a <Fourier sine series> in normalized form. For a square-integrable $f$, completeness gives convergence in the norm of the function space; pointwise convergence requires the usual additional regularity. If $f$ is a normalized <wavefunction>, the <Born rule> gives probabilities $|a_n|^2$ and $\sum_n|a_n|^2=1$.

= b
{parent=A1}
