= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Set $x=r\phi$ and $a=2\pi r$. The wrapped spatial form is
$$
\Psi_n(r\phi)=\sqrt{\frac2a}\sin\frac{n\phi}{2},\qquad
\frac{\partial^2\Psi_n}{\partial\phi^2}=-\frac{n^2}4\Psi_n.
$$
Hence the differential expression for the <quantum rotor> acts as
$$
\hat H_{\mathrm R}\Psi_n=\frac{n^2\hbar^2}{8I}\Psi_n.
$$
The box expression becomes $E_n=n^2\hbar^2/(8mr^2)$, so consistency gives
$$
\boxed{I=mr^2,}
$$
the <moment of inertia> of a point mass a distance $r$ from the axis. Equivalently, $\partial_x=r^{-1}\partial_\phi$ transforms the kinetic operator directly. Being an eigenfunction of this differential expression is not yet sufficient: the ring boundary conditions must also hold.

For a wavefunction normalized with the angular measure $\mathrm d\phi$, use $\Phi_n(\phi)=\sqrt r\,\Psi_n(r\phi)=\pi^{-1/2}\sin(n\phi/2)$. This change of normalization convention does not change the <energy eigenvalue>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A smooth <quantum rotor> with no flux or twist requires both
$$
\Phi(\phi+2\pi)=\Phi(\phi),\qquad
\Phi'(\phi+2\pi)=\Phi'(\phi).
$$
For the sine family, $\Phi_n(0)=\Phi_n(2\pi)=0$ holds for every positive integer $n$, but
$$
\Phi_n'(0)=\frac{n}{2\sqrt\pi},\qquad
\Phi_n'(2\pi)=\frac{n}{2\sqrt\pi}(-1)^n.
$$
The derivatives match only for **$\boxed{n=2k,\quad k=1,2,3,\ldots}$**. Equivalently, $\sin[n(\phi+2\pi)/2]=(-1)^n\sin(n\phi/2)$ must be periodic for every $\phi$, not merely vanish at the chosen join. Odd $n$ would produce a cusp in a periodic continuation and do not belong to the smooth free-ring Hamiltonian's domain.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Solving $-i\hbar u'(\phi)=\lambda u(\phi)$ gives an exponential. The <periodic boundary condition> quantizes its phase winding:
$$
\boxed{u_k(\phi)=\frac{e^{ik\phi}}{\sqrt{2\pi}},\quad
\hat l_z u_k=\hbar k u_k,\quad k\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
These are normalized with respect to $\mathrm d\phi$. The corresponding complex <Fourier series> is
$$
\boxed{f(\phi)=\sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty}c_k u_k(\phi),\qquad
c_k=\int_0^{2\pi}u_k(\phi)^*f(\phi)\,\mathrm d\phi.}
$$
Unlike the wrapped sine subset, this <orthonormal basis> includes the constant $k=0$ state and both sine and cosine combinations. If one retains arc-length normalization instead, replace $1/\sqrt{2\pi}$ by $1/\sqrt a$ and integrate with $r\,\mathrm d\phi$.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For the allowed $n=2k$ states, the normalized standing wave is a <quantum superposition> of two opposite <angular momentum eigenstates>:
$$
\Phi_{2k}(\phi)=\frac{\sin(k\phi)}{\sqrt\pi}
=\frac{u_k(\phi)-u_{-k}(\phi)}{i\sqrt2}.
$$
The <Born rule> therefore predicts
$$
\boxed{l_z=+k\hbar\ \text{or}\ -k\hbar,\qquad P(+k\hbar)=P(-k\hbar)=\tfrac12.}
$$
The <expectation value> is zero, but a single measurement never gives zero for these $k\ge1$ states. Their uncertainty is $\Delta l_z=k\hbar$. The two components have the same rotor energy $\hbar^2k^2/(2I)$, so a definite energy does not imply definite angular momentum.

= A2
{c}
{parent=Paper 1}
