= a
{parent=A2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Differentiate the Gaussian before substituting into the <Hamiltonian (quantum mechanics)>:
$$
\Psi_0'=-\alpha x\Psi_0,\qquad
\Psi_0''=(\alpha^2x^2-\alpha)\Psi_0.
$$
Thus
$$
\hat H\Psi_0=\left[\frac{\hbar^2\alpha}{2m}
+\left(\frac{m\omega^2}2-\frac{\hbar^2\alpha^2}{2m}\right)x^2\right]\Psi_0.
$$
An <energy eigenvalue> must be independent of $x$, so the coefficient of $x^2$ vanishes. Normalizability requires $\alpha>0$, giving
$$
\boxed{\alpha=\frac{m\omega}{\hbar},\qquad E_0=\frac12\hbar\omega.}
$$
The constant term is the <quantum harmonic oscillator>'s zero-point energy; a Gaussian width $\alpha^{-1/2}$ balances its kinetic and potential contributions.

= b
{parent=A2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a normalized state and a Hermitian <quantum observable> $\hat A$, its <quantum uncertainty> is the standard deviation of repeated measurement outcomes:
$$
\boxed{\Delta A=\sqrt{\langle(\hat A-\langle A\rangle)^2\rangle}
=\sqrt{\langle A^2\rangle-\langle A\rangle^2}.}
$$
Here $\langle A\rangle=\langle\Psi|\hat A|\Psi\rangle$. It is a property of the state and observable, not an instrumental error; the required moments must be finite.

= c
{parent=A2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

In the position representation, $\hat x$ multiplies by $x$ and $\hat p_x=-i\hbar\,\mathrm d/\mathrm dx$. Acting on an arbitrary differentiable test function $f$ shows the <commutator> explicitly:
$$
\begin{aligned}
[\hat x,\hat p_x]f
&=-i\hbar x f'+i\hbar\frac{\mathrm d(xf)}{\mathrm dx}\\
&=-i\hbar xf'+i\hbar(f+xf')=i\hbar f.
\end{aligned}
$$
Therefore $[\hat x,\hat p_x]=i\hbar\hat 1$, whose <expectation value> is $i\hbar$ in every normalized state in the relevant domains. The <Robertson uncertainty principle> gives
$$
\boxed{\Delta x\,\Delta p_x\ge\frac{\hbar}{2}.}
$$
This is a lower bound, not a claim that every state saturates it.

= d
{parent=A2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The density $|\Psi_0|^2=\sqrt{\alpha/\pi}\,e^{-\alpha x^2}$ is even. Hence $\langle x\rangle=0$; also $\Psi_0'=-\alpha x\Psi_0$ gives $\langle p_x\rangle=i\hbar\alpha\langle x\rangle=0$.

To evaluate the second position moment, differentiate the <Gaussian integral> with respect to its positive parameter:
$$
\begin{aligned}
G(\alpha)&=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-\alpha x^2}\,\mathrm dx=\sqrt\pi\,\alpha^{-1/2},\\
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}x^2e^{-\alpha x^2}\,\mathrm dx
&=-G'(\alpha)=\frac{\sqrt\pi}{2\alpha^{3/2}},\\
\langle x^2\rangle&=\sqrt{\frac\alpha\pi}\frac{\sqrt\pi}{2\alpha^{3/2}}
=\frac1{2\alpha}=\frac{\hbar}{2m\omega}.
\end{aligned}
$$
For the momentum second moment, apply the <momentum operator> twice:
$$
\langle p_x^2\rangle=-\hbar^2\int\Psi_0^*\Psi_0''\,\mathrm dx
=\hbar^2\left(\alpha-\alpha^2\langle x^2\rangle\right)
=\frac{\hbar^2\alpha}{2}=\frac{m\hbar\omega}{2}.
$$
Both means vanish, so
$$
\boxed{\Delta x=\sqrt{\frac{\hbar}{2m\omega}},\qquad
\Delta p_x=\sqrt{\frac{m\hbar\omega}{2}},\qquad
\Delta x\,\Delta p_x=\frac\hbar2.}
$$
The <ground state> attains the bound proved above and is therefore a <minimum uncertainty state>. A wider Gaussian reduces momentum spread while increasing position spread: the product remains at this lower limit. Other states, including excited oscillator eigenstates, generally give a larger product.

= e
{parent=A2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Write $\hat N=\hat a^\dagger\hat a$. The <creation and annihilation operators> obey
$$
[\hat N,\hat a^\dagger]
=\hat a^\dagger[\hat a,\hat a^\dagger]=\hat a^\dagger,
\qquad
[\hat H,\hat a^\dagger]=\hbar\omega\hat a^\dagger.
$$
Consequently,
$$
\begin{aligned}
\hat H\hat a^\dagger\Psi_0
&=(\hat a^\dagger\hat H+[\hat H,\hat a^\dagger])\Psi_0\\
&=(E_0+\hbar\omega)\hat a^\dagger\Psi_0
=\boxed{\frac32\hbar\omega\,\hat a^\dagger\Psi_0}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The resulting function is not zero: its squared norm is
$$
\|\hat a^\dagger\Psi_0\|^2
=\langle\Psi_0|\hat a\hat a^\dagger|\Psi_0\rangle
=\langle\hat a^\dagger\hat a+1\rangle=1.
$$
Thus **$\hat a^\dagger\Psi_0$ is the normalized first excited state**, up to phase. More generally the <creation operator> raises the energy by one quantum $\hbar\omega$: $\hat a^\dagger|n\rangle=\sqrt{n+1}\,|n+1\rangle$. The factor is required because applying it to a higher state does not preserve normalization.

