= Solution
{parent=i}

A polar diatomic molecule can absorb microwaves through its permanent <electric dipole moment>. In the <linear rigid rotor> approximation,
$$
E_J=hB_0J(J+1),\quad J=0,1,2,\ldots,
\qquad B_0=\frac{h}{8\pi^2I_0}.
$$
The electric-dipole <selection rule> is $\Delta J=+1$ in absorption. It gives
$$
\nu_{J\to J+1}=\frac{E_{J+1}-E_J}{h}=2B_0(J+1),
$$
so adjacent lines are separated by $2B_0$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{B_0=\frac{21.999}{2}=10.9995\ \mathrm{GHz}.}
$$
Assign a line near $m\times21.999\ \mathrm{GHz}$ to $J=m-1\to m$ for positive integer $m$. The first member is $0\to1$, the next $1\to2$, and so on; if the recorded window misses the lowest lines, their absolute frequencies fix the integer offset. At high temperature many rotational states are populated, so the strongest line need not be $0\to1$.

This is the leading rotational pattern, not a claim that real ZnF lacks spin or hyperfine structure. Fine splittings and <centrifugal distortion> can perturb the ideal lines; they do not explain the three systematically displaced progressions as economically as vibrational excitation.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The two extra progressions are **rotational transitions within thermally populated $v=1$ and $v=2$ vibrational levels**. Isotopic purity rules out attributing them to different isotope masses. Their <rotational constants> are
$$
\boxed{B_1=10.9115\ \mathrm{GHz},\qquad B_2=10.8235\ \mathrm{GHz}.}
$$
The <Boltzmann distribution> makes each higher vibrational manifold less populated, so its rotational spectrum is weaker. A <Morse oscillator> explores larger separations as it is excited: the bond's vibrationally averaged moment of inertia increases and the rotational spacing decreases. More precisely, $B_v$ involves an average of the inverse square separation; it is not simply the inverse square of the mean separation.

The observed decrement is constant to the given precision:
$$
B_0-B_1=B_1-B_2=\boxed{\alpha_e=0.0880\ \mathrm{GHz}}.
$$
This fits the leading <vibration-rotation interaction> law $B_v=B_e-\alpha_e(v+\tfrac12)$.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

An <equilibrium bond length> requires $B_e$, not merely the ground-vibrational-state constant. Extrapolating the linear <vibration-rotation interaction> law gives
$$
B_e=B_0+\frac{\alpha_e}{2}=10.9995+0.0440
=\boxed{11.0435\ \mathrm{GHz}}.
$$
Using integer isotope masses, the <reduced mass> is
$$
\mu=\frac{64\times19}{64+19}\,u=14.65060\,u
=2.43279\times10^{-26}\ \mathrm{kg}.
$$
For two nuclei $I_e=\mu r_e^2$, so
$$
\boxed{r_e=\sqrt{\frac{h}{8\pi^2\mu B_e}}
=1.76737\times10^{-10}\ \mathrm m\simeq176.7\ \mathrm{pm}.}
$$
Using $B_0$ instead gives $r_0\simeq177.1$ pm, an effective vibrational separation. The small difference is precisely why the weaker vibrational progressions matter when the target is equilibrium geometry. Higher terms in $B_v$ and the rigid-rotor approximation limit this estimate's precision.

