The <molecular symmetry operations> must be closed under composition. Write the proposed nonidentity operations as $R=C_2$ and $S=\sigma$. Their product $RS$ cannot equal any proposed member:
$$
RS=E\Rightarrow R=S^{-1}=S,\qquad
RS=R\Rightarrow S=E,\qquad
RS=S\Rightarrow R=E.
$$
Each conclusion contradicts the distinct operations stipulated. Therefore **at least a fourth symmetry operation must exist**. For a mirror plane containing the twofold axis, the product generates another mirror plane; for a plane perpendicular to it, the product is inversion. A tilted plane may generate still more operations, but cannot evade group closure.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

One choice is **the <C2v molecular point group>**, $\{E,C_2(z),\sigma_{xz},\sigma_{yz}\}$. The additional operation is a second reflection, since
$$
C_2(z)\sigma_{xz}=\sigma_{yz}.
$$
A bent <water> molecule has this symmetry. Choose the $z$ axis along its bond-angle bisector and put the molecule in the $xz$ plane: reflection in $yz$ exchanges its two hydrogen atoms, while reflection in $xz$ fixes their positions.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

With $R=C_2(z)$, $S=\sigma_{xz}$ and $T=\sigma_{yz}$, composition gives
$$
\begin{array}{c|cccc}
 &E&R&S&T\\\hline
E&E&R&S&T\\
R&R&E&T&S\\
S&S&T&E&R\\
T&T&S&R&E
\end{array}
$$
Every entry belongs to the set, proving closure. $E$ is the <identity element>; every element is its own inverse. Associativity follows because these are actual spatial transformations: composing functions is associative. Thus all group axioms hold, and the group is isomorphic to the <Klein four-group>.

For a concrete matrix check, use $E=\operatorname{diag}(1,1,1)$, $R=\operatorname{diag}(-1,-1,1)$, $S=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,1)$ and $T=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1)$. Their matrix products reproduce the table.

= b
{parent=A4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A **<symmetry-closed orbital basis>**, often called a complete basis in this context, is a linearly independent set whose span is carried into itself by every group operation. No transformed basis function requires a function outside that span. This is completeness for the chosen <group representation>, not a claim that a small atomic-orbital set spans the full electronic Hilbert space.

A **representation matrix** $D(g)$ specifies the linear transformation produced by a <molecular symmetry operation> $g$. With the column convention,
$$
\hat U(g)\phi_j=\sum_i\phi_iD_{ij}(g),\qquad
D(gh)=D(g)D(h).
$$
Its **<character of a representation>** is the <matrix trace>,
$$
\boxed{\chi(g)=\operatorname{tr}D(g)=\sum_iD_{ii}(g).}
$$
The character is unchanged by a simultaneous change of basis and is constant on a <conjugacy class>, which is why character tables have one column per class.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the ordered basis $(p_x,p_y,p_z)$ at the origin. Choose $z$ as the principal improper-rotation axis, $x,y$ along the two $C_2^\prime$ axes, and the diagonal mirror planes as $x=\pm y$. Use active operations with $\hat U(g)f(\mathbf r)=f(g^{-1}\mathbf r)$, so the matrix columns give the transformed orbitals. For these real p orbitals the matrices are the ordinary vector transformation matrices.

One representative from each <D2d molecular point group> class is
$$
\begin{aligned}
D(E)&=\begin{pmatrix}1&0&0\\0&1&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix},\\
D(S_4^+)&=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1&0\\1&0&0\\0&0&-1\end{pmatrix},\\
D(C_2(z))&=\begin{pmatrix}-1&0&0\\0&-1&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix},\\
D(C_2(x))&=\begin{pmatrix}1&0&0\\0&-1&0\\0&0&-1\end{pmatrix},\\
D(\sigma_{x=y})&=\begin{pmatrix}0&1&0\\1&0&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}
$$
For example $S_4^+$ sends $p_x\mapsto p_y$, $p_y\mapsto-p_x$ and $p_z\mapsto-p_z$. Choosing the opposite sense of $S_4$ transposes its xy block but leaves every character and the final decomposition unchanged.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Taking the <matrix trace> of those matrices gives
$$
\begin{array}{c|rrrrr}
\text{class}&E&2S_4&C_2&2C_2^\prime&2\sigma_d\\\hline
\chi_p&3&-1&-1&-1&1
\end{array}
$$
The one-dimensional $p_z$ subspace has characters $(1,-1,1,-1,1)$, identifying $B_2$. The pair $(p_x,p_y)$ has characters $(2,0,-2,0,0)$, identifying $E$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\Gamma_{p_x,p_y,p_z}=B_2\oplus E.}
$$
The dimensions add to three, as required. The $p_x,p_y$ pair must be retained together: a ninety-degree improper rotation mixes them, so either one alone is not a complete symmetry basis.<Tables for Group Theory by P. W. Atkins and others>[Printed page 12, $D_{2d}$ vector functions]{ref}

